# Docker Compose reads .env from the repository root. Copy this file to .env
# before changing any defaults; do not commit the resulting file.

# Published ports bind to loopback by default. Set AOS_UI_BIND_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0
# only on a trusted private network, then use browser-reachable URLs/origins.
AOS_UI_BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1
AOS_UI_WEB_PUBLISHED_PORT=3000
# Caddy's MCP App sandbox site, one for both lanes and part of neither, with a
# bind address of its own. It serves only the sandbox page, its script, and the
# stable hosts below; make it public when the guest lane is.
AOS_UI_SANDBOX_BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1
AOS_UI_SANDBOX_PUBLISHED_PORT=3002

# Compose mounts this public configuration into the web container. Fixture
# mode is the safe backend-free default. The browser's other mode is `aos`,
# which reaches the harness-gw gateway through Caddy; provider URLs stay there.

# Caddy answers only these Host names (plus 127.0.0.1 and localhost) and gives
# any other a 421. Set the name the browser uses, such as a tailnet name; never
# set localhost or 127.0.0.1 here, which Caddy already lists.
# AOS_UI_PUBLIC_HOST=aos.example.test
# AOS_UI_GUEST_PUBLIC_HOST=guest.example.test

# Every exact origin people open AOS UI at, operator and guest alike, as the
# browser's address bar shows them and comma-separated, and the one MCP App
# sandbox origin every view loads from: any origin that differs from all of
# them, such as another name or a lane's name on another port (on loopback, a
# name below localhost), which Caddy's sandbox site sends to the web server
# whole. The web server refuses to start without them; Compose defaults them
# to loopback on the published ports, the sandbox on
# AOS_UI_SANDBOX_PUBLISHED_PORT, or on Caddy's own ports under
# deploy/compose.host.yaml, the sandbox on 18082. Never list the sandbox origin
# in the gateway's allowed origins.
# AOS_UI_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://aos.example.test,https://guest.example.test
# AOS_UI_MCP_APP_SANDBOX_ORIGIN=https://mcp-apps.example.com

# Optional, and off while unset: the MCP App stable origins, one wildcard
# origin <scheme>://*.<domain>[:port]. A view that asks for a domain of exactly
# one 32-hex-digit key label directly below <domain> (see docs/mcp-apps.md)
# loads from that host, with this scheme and port, instead of the shared
# sandbox; Caddy's sandbox site sends each such host to the web server whole,
# and any other name at or below <domain> is refused. The sandbox may be
# <domain> itself, as in these examples. The web server refuses to start
# unless no lane's host is at or below <domain>, the sandbox's is not below
# it, the wildcard is https: when any lane is, and loopback exactly when the
# lanes are (on loopback, a name below localhost on the sandbox site's port,
# such as http://*.mcp-apps.localhost:3002). Outside loopback, every
# <key>.<domain> needs DNS and a certificate that covers it, and so does
# <domain> when it is the sandbox: a wildcard does not cover its own apex.
# AOS_UI_MCP_APP_STABLE_ORIGINS=https://*.mcp-apps.example.com
AOS_UI_RUNTIME_CONFIG_FILE=./deploy/runtime-config.fixture.json

# The gateway container stays non-root. On Linux, set these to the numeric
# owner of the mounted secret files (`id -u` and `id -g` for user-owned files).
# Local Compose bind-mounts secrets without changing host ownership. OpenCode
# also uses these IDs for its worktree and mode-600 AWS files. The defaults suit
# hosts whose user/group IDs are 1000.
AOS_UI_HOST_UID=1000
AOS_UI_HOST_GID=1000

# Used only by the retained native OpenCode server-adapter overlay. The
# container mounts this external harness worktree at /workspace; it is not
# this UI checkout and is not a browser runtime.
AOS_UI_OPENCODE_WORKTREE=/absolute/path/to/opencode-worktree

# Compose mounts ${HOME}/.aws read-only by default. Use this when your AWS
# profile or SSO config lives elsewhere; credentials and region pass through.
# AOS_UI_AWS_CONFIG_DIR=/absolute/path/to/.aws
# AWS_PROFILE=your-profile
# AWS_REGION=eu-central-1
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# AWS_SESSION_TOKEN=

# Google models. OpenCode reads GOOGLE_GENERATIVE_AI_API_KEY; the launcher also
# accepts GEMINI_API_KEY as an alias when the canonical variable is unset.
# GOOGLE_GENERATIVE_AI_API_KEY=
# GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Optional OpenAI-compatible provider. All three are required together. It is
# exposed to OpenCode as provider "openai-compatible" and model "default".
# AOS_UI_OPENAI_COMPATIBLE_BASE_URL=https://models.example.test/v1
# AOS_UI_OPENAI_COMPATIBLE_API_KEY=replace-me
# AOS_UI_OPENAI_COMPATIBLE_MODEL_ID=upstream-model-id

# Hermes is operator-managed. Its native server must be reachable from the
# gateway container; host loopback alone is not reachable through Docker.
# Copy harness-gw's examples/config.hermes.example.yaml outside the checkout,
# customize its listener origins and Hermes address, and keep it owner-only.
# Compose bind-mounts this file without changing host ownership, so it must be
# owner-only-writable and owned by AOS_UI_HOST_UID or root. HARNESS_GW_IMAGE
# overrides the pinned gateway image.
# HARNESS_GW_CONFIG_FILE=/absolute/private/path/harness-gw.yaml
# A single server token authenticates the shared Hermes runtime used by both
# the trusted operator listener and the optional scoped guest listener.
# HARNESS_GW_HERMES_TOKEN_FILE=/absolute/private/path/hermes-token
# Guests use a separate port and scoped JWTs. Keep the signing key private; the
# guest origin and listener belong in the gateway config.
# HARNESS_GW_GUEST_INVITE_SIGNING_KEY_FILE=/absolute/private/path/guest-invite-signing-key
# AOS_UI_GUEST_BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1
# AOS_UI_GUEST_PUBLISHED_PORT=3001

# OpenCode overlay. The public runtime config remains {"mode":"aos"}; this
# private config starts from harness-gw's examples/config.opencode.example.yaml
# and selects
# the internal OpenCode service and its fixed workspace/Basic-auth identity. The
# password is mounted into both the gateway and native service as a file; never
# put the password value in this file.
# AOS_UI_RUNTIME_CONFIG_FILE=./deploy/runtime-config.opencode.json
# HARNESS_GW_CONFIG_FILE=/absolute/private/path/harness-gw.opencode.yaml
# HARNESS_GW_OPENCODE_PASSWORD_FILE=/absolute/private/path/opencode-password

# OpenClaw overlay. OpenClaw is independently operated and reachable from the
# gateway through the private ws/wss URL in its config, copied from harness-gw's
# examples/config.openclaw.example.yaml. Device identity and token are mounted
# as owner-only files; do not pass a Gateway host, port, token, or device
# material to the browser environment.
# AOS_UI_RUNTIME_CONFIG_FILE=./deploy/runtime-config.openclaw.json
# HARNESS_GW_CONFIG_FILE=/absolute/private/path/harness-gw.openclaw.yaml
# HARNESS_GW_OPENCLAW_DEVICE_IDENTITY_FILE=/absolute/private/path/openclaw-device-identity
# HARNESS_GW_OPENCLAW_DEVICE_TOKEN_FILE=/absolute/private/path/openclaw-device-token

# Web Push (optional). Used only when adding -f compose.push.yaml to the
# runtime overlay command; that overlay passes these settings to the gateway
# as container environment variables, so the private gateway config needs no
# push section. The state directory must exist and be writable by the gateway user
# before starting. The VAPID private key is a 43-character base64url scalar;
# generate one with:
#   bunx web-push generate-vapid-keys
# and keep only the private key in the file (mode 0600). The subject is the
# operator contact sent to push services (mailto: or https:).
# HARNESS_GW_PUSH_STATE_DIR=/var/lib/harness-gw/push
# HARNESS_GW_PUSH_VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE=/absolute/private/path/vapid-private-key
# HARNESS_GW_PUSH_VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:ops@example.com

# The UI's tools MCP server (render_chart, render_map, render_stats,
# present_artifact). It has no authentication and is always published on host
# loopback, whatever AOS_UI_BIND_ADDRESS says; register
# http://127.0.0.1:${AOS_UI_TOOLS_MCP_PORT}/mcp in a harness on this host.
# Compose services reach it at http://tools-mcp:4110/mcp: the OpenCode overlay
# registers that URL, and the Hermes gateway example overrides the loopback URL a
# host Hermes registers with it (mcpApps.fallback.servers.aos-ui.url).
AOS_UI_TOOLS_MCP_PORT=4110
# How the tools server reaches each lane for file reads, when the lane's own
# origin is not directly reachable from inside the container. Format:
# <lane origin>=<base URL>, comma-separated. Bridged Compose sets this from
# the published-port origins to the internal Caddy addresses; host networking
# leaves it empty. Set it explicitly only when running outside Compose in a
# network where the lane origin is not reachable directly.
# AOS_UI_TOOLS_MCP_LANE_UPSTREAMS=http://localhost:3000=http://caddy:18080,http://127.0.0.1:3000=http://caddy:18080
# With AOS_UI_MCP_APP_STABLE_ORIGINS set, the URL the gateway dials this server
# at, which names the artifact view's stable origin. Each shape defaults it to
# the URL its gateway dials: http://tools-mcp:4110/mcp under the Hermes and
# OpenCode overlays, and otherwise, host networking included, the loopback URL a
# harness on the host registers. Set it when the gateway's configuration dials
# another one.
# AOS_UI_TOOLS_MCP_URL=http://tools-mcp:4110/mcp
